### Serve a Page from a Host Folder

#### Task
Your team keeps a small static landing page in a folder on the host and wants nginx to serve it directly, so editing the file on the host updates the live page with no rebuild. A bind mount is exactly the tool.

#### Instructions
1. On the host, create the folder `/root/binddemo` and inside it an `index.html` whose content includes the exact line `served from a bind mount`.
2. Start an nginx container in the background named `bindweb` from the `nginx:alpine` image. Publish the container's port `80` to host port `8085`, and bind-mount the host folder `/root/binddemo` onto the nginx web root `/usr/share/nginx/html`.
3. When it works, a request to `http://localhost:8085` from the host returns your page.

---

```bash
# Pull the lightweight Alpine-based Nginx image from Docker Hub
docker pull nginx:alpine

# Create the directory on the host where the HTML file will live
mkdir -p /root/binddemo

# Add the required content into index.html inside the newly created folder
echo 'served from a bind mount' > /root/binddemo/index.html

# Run the Nginx container in detached (background) mode
# - --name bindweb: Names the container 'bindweb'
# - -p 8085:80: Maps host port 8085 to container port 80
# - -v /root/binddemo:/usr/share/nginx/html: Bind-mounts host folder to Nginx root directory
docker run -d --name bindweb -p 8085:80 \
  -v /root/binddemo:/usr/share/nginx/html nginx:alpine

# Retry loop: Check up to 10 times to verify the server is responding correctly
for i in $(seq 1 10); do
  # Perform a silent request to the host port and check if the expected text is returned
  curl -s --max-time 2 http://localhost:8085 | grep -q 'served from a bind mount' && break
  sleep 1
done

# Final check to fetch and print the output from the running Nginx container
curl -s --max-time 5 http://localhost:8085
